## Blocking findings

  1. A3 does not preserve all SSH launch/save paths

  The proposed resolve_launch_password(saved_session_id, supplied) is insufficient.

  - The saved-target picker deliberately clears saved_session_id while currently borrowing that preset’s password (/C:/
    Users/s.zupanec/Desktop/gridvibe_colab/web/static/js/launcher.js:1216). After redaction, this launch would lose
    password authentication.

  - Both launch builders convert "" to null (/C:/Users/s.zupanec/Desktop/gridvibe_colab/web/static/js/launcher.js:3600, /
    C:/Users/s.zupanec/Desktop/gridvibe_colab/web/static/js/terminals.js:1833). That destroys the distinction between
    “explicitly clear” and “unstated”.

  - Re-saving an imported preset with an untouched blank field would overwrite its stored password with empty.
  - Reusing a preset password solely by ID could send the old secret to a newly edited host/user/port. Existing restore
    code correctly binds credentials to all three target fields (/C:/Users/s.zupanec/Desktop/gridvibe_colab/web/
    workspaces.py:1050); A3 must use the same rule.

  - SSH agent preflight also needs the stored credential. It currently receives the form password, so password-only hosts
    would show failed preflight after redaction.

  - The workspace page would emit a false “No password is saved” warning because it inspects the now-redacted payload (/C:/
    Users/s.zupanec/Desktop/gridvibe_colab/web/static/js/terminals.js:1948).

  - The DELETE response returns a config outside _saved_session_response() and must also be redacted (/C:/Users/s.zupanec/
    Desktop/gridvibe_colab/web/api.py:2763).

  A safer contract is:

  resolve_ssh_credential(
      credential_reference,
      host,
      username,
      port,
      supplied=MISSING,
  )

  - MISSING: use referenced stored password only when the target matches.
  - "": deliberately use/ save no password.
  - Non-empty: use the supplied replacement.
  - Keep credential_reference separate from persisted saved_session_id, because the target picker must remain a scratch
    launch.

  - Apply this contract to launch, agent preflight, preset overwrite, preset clone/Save As, and target-picker launch.
  - Keep the password state in JS state, not only a DOM data-* marker, because renderModeFields() replaces the password
    element.

  2. Flask before_request does not cover Socket.IO transport requests

  A1 places Host validation in a Flask hook. Flask-SocketIO’s middleware handles /socket.io before Flask routing, so that
  hook does not protect the Engine.IO handshake.

  Use either:

  - an outer WSGI Host-validation middleware wrapping both Flask and Socket.IO, or
  - an explicit canonical Host check in the Socket.IO connect handler as well.

  Add hostile-Host Socket.IO cases with both a matching Origin and no Origin.

  3. trusted_proxy: bool is not a safe trust boundary

  When enabled, any client reaching the backend directly can still supply X-Forwarded-*. The setting must either:

  - include trusted proxy addresses/CIDRs and an explicit hop policy, with request.remote_addr verification, or
  - be removed for now and require proxies to preserve/configure Host and cors_origins explicitly.

  4. The /auth?token=… bootstrap leaks the capability

  The URL can enter Werkzeug/proxy access logs, browser history, and the browser-launch process command line. That
  conflicts with “the token never reaches the rotating log”.

  Prefer /auth#token=… with a small page that POSTs the fragment value, or a manual password-style POST form. The fragment
  never reaches HTTP logs.

  Native mode also needs explicit handling:

  - _wait_for_server() currently performs an unauthenticated health request (/C:/Users/s.zupanec/Desktop/gridvibe_colab/
    web/webview_launcher.py:1636).

  - Native download/upload bridge requests use independent urlopen() calls without browser cookies (/C:/Users/s.zupanec/
    Desktop/gridvibe_colab/web/webview_launcher.py:1092).

  - Launcher and workspace WebViews need an authenticated cookie bootstrap.

  5. Security config cannot safely inherit the current custom-config split

  main.py imports the global application before parsing --config (/C:/Users/s.zupanec/Desktop/gridvibe_colab/main.py:24).
  The entry point can therefore gate startup using one file while web.app reads Host/token settings from another.

  A1/A2 need one resolved immutable security state passed from the entry point. Deferring the general GV-017 cleanup is
  reasonable, but the security boundary itself cannot be split-brain.

  6. Host/origin configuration needs clearer semantics

  The document currently leaves several contradictions:

  - allowed_hosts is described as “extra”, but derived defaults are stated only when it is empty.
  - An explicit proxy cors_origins entry does not automatically become an allowed Host, so previously sufficient reverse-
    proxy configuration may start returning 403.

  - A wildcard bind plus remote opt-in but no named allowed host starts successfully while remaining unreachable remotely.
  - cors_origins: ["*"] should not disable Host validation. It may retain its existing meaning for CORS/origin checks
    without also trusting arbitrary Host headers.

  - The immutable generation must be captured once, such as in Flask g; two separate hooks otherwise can read different
    generations.

  For wildcard binding, startup should require at least one explicit reachable authority from allowed_hosts or a concrete
  configured origin.

  7. A4 omits events explicitly covered by GV-007

  The source audit includes voice events, but A4’s matrix covers only terminal join/input/resize/clear. It must also cover:

  - leave_session
  - voice_start, voice_audio, and voice_stop
  - membership/ownership for audio and stop
  - audio size and per-client/global recording caps
  - integer/type bounds for resize

  Also, the existing _MAX_TRACKED_SOCKET_CLIENTS eviction cannot remain out of scope if client_joined_sessions becomes the
  authorization source: eviction would revoke a still-connected client’s authority. Use a dedicated non-evicting live
  membership registry or fix the lifecycle/cap as part of A4.

  8. A5 would break external images in Markdown previews

  The proposed img-src 'self' data: blob: blocks existing sanitized Markdown such as:

  <img src="https://example.com/image.png">

  GridVibe explicitly permits those image tags today (/C:/Users/s.zupanec/Desktop/gridvibe_colab/web/explorer.py:310). I
  confirmed the current renderer preserves an HTTPS image.

  To preserve behavior, include http: https: in img-src, or make blocking remote Markdown images an explicit product change
  with UI/tests.

  9. The “16 tests unmodified” promise is unrealistic

  Several current tests use the test client’s implicit Host: localhost and origins without the configured :5050 port. A
  correct full-origin policy should reject those combinations.

  Preserve the behavioral verdicts, but update the tests to supply an explicit honest Host/base URL. Keeping them literally
  unmodified would require unsafe origin equivalence.

  10. Remote-token mode must be described as trusted-LAN-only

  Without TLS, the token and cookie are observable on the network. A2 is still a major improvement, but it should not imply
  safe public/internet exposure. The startup posture should say “authenticated, unencrypted—trusted network or TLS proxy
  required.”

  ## SSH save/restore conclusion

  The existing runtime restore architecture is good: snapshots remain password-free, attached credentials are target-
  matched, scratch target restores use an unambiguous target lookup, and ambiguous credentials are not guessed.

  I ran 33 existing tests:

  - all 25 current origin/CORS tests;
  - eight key SSH save/restore and credential-matching tests.

  All passed.

  Those restore paths can remain unaffected, but A3 as currently written would regress:

  - target-picker scratch launches;
  - password-only agent preflight;
  - imported-preset overwrite and Save As;
  - explicit blank-password selection;
  - workspace-page warning accuracy.

  Add those cases to the A3 matrix, along with native-mode authentication and all response methods—not just GET.